* UPGRADE_NOCASE
* RELEASE_1.1 5 D2.  Convert an upgraded machine's existing hashed files to
* case insensitive ids, so the owner's ruling - no two record ids may differ
* only by case, anywhere - holds on a machine that predates the change.
*
* A FRESH install already makes every file NOCASE (gplsrc/op_dio1.c), so this
* is the UPGRADE path only.  An upgrade preserves each account's data files,
* which were built case sensitive, so each must be converted with the same
* rebuild CONFIGURE.FILE uses.
*
* ***SAFETY: A SCAN PHASE FIRST.  NOTHING IS REBUILT UNTIL IT IS PROVEN CLEAN.***
* Owner's instruction, 14 Sep 2026: pre-check and warn if potential duplicates
* are found.  Phase 1 opens every file read only and looks for a fold-pair -
* two ids differing only by case.  A file that HOLDS such a pair is NOT
* converted (a rebuild to NOCASE would keep only one - the loss this whole
* change exists to prevent); it is named in a warning for the administrator to
* resolve by hand and convert with CONFIGURE.FILE.  Only files proven to hold
* no pair are rebuilt in phase 2, so a conversion can never lose a record.
*
* RUN gpl.bp upgrade_nocase          scan and convert the clean files
* RUN gpl.bp upgrade_nocase CHECK    scan and report only; convert nothing
*
* It runs as sd -internal in SDSYS (the installer's upgrade step), walking the
* account register by path.  It never LOGTOs another account: a file is opened
* by its pathname, converted, and swapped, exactly as CONFIGURE.FILE's
* convert.file does - which is why converting a VOC nobody is logged into is
* safe.  SDSYS is not walked at all: its live VOC is the one file this must
* never rebuild (the session runs from it).
*
* SD CORE SOLO FOR WINDOWS.  Linux Solo's program in SD Core for Windows' Windows form.  What differs from SD Core
* for Windows, each forced by Solo's one-user model (as Linux Solo has it):
*   1. OWNERSHIP.  A rebuilt file is a new file, owned by whoever makes it.  Solo's one account belongs to the
*      user who runs SD, and this program runs as that user, so every new file is the account owner's already.
*   2. NO "OUTSIDE THE ACCOUNT" RULE.  SD Core leaves a file a VOC names outside its account (the walk runs
*      elevated there, and it would rebuild somebody else's file).  Solo has one user and its files all belong
*      to that user, so such a file is converted.  A file under SDSYS that a VOC names is still left, counted
*      and named at the end (15993, Linux Solo 14993 + 1000): it is not the account's to rebuild, and SDSYS's
*      own files are made new by the install.
* And what differs from Linux Solo, forced by Windows: NTFS folds case, so the private catalogue's cat/ names
* need no renaming step (Linux Solo's fix.cat.names), and paths are compared with one separator and upper
* cased, because @sdsys carries backslashes where @ds is '/'.
*
* START-HISTORY:
* 21 Nov 26 SD Core Solo - as Linux Solo c2d5f21 (S.296, Windows row 377): a file under SDSYS that is already case insensitive is not counted or
*           named (a read only open reads FL$FLAGS); one that is not, or cannot be opened, still is.
* 17 Nov 26 SD Core Solo - four-version audit round 4 (P-7894): the VOC's own conversion keeps Linux Solo's "if not(this.is.sdsys)"
*           guard.  Same result: this.is.sdsys is false in the Solo walk.
* 18 Nov 26 SD Core Solo - four-version audit round 3 (P-5661): carried from SD Core for Windows' program (17 Nov 26) in Linux
*           Solo's shape: SDSYS is skipped, each account walked is named (12035), an unopenable file is reported (12013) and
*           counted, a file under SDSYS that a VOC names is left and named (15993), and there is no outside-the-account rule
*           (one user owns every file).  Solo removed its case fallbacks in round 2 (5d8cd25f), so an upgrade over an older
*           Solo with mixed-case ids needs this walk to find them again.  Messages 12013, 12035 and 15993 come with it.
* END-HISTORY

program UPGRADE_NOCASE
$internal
$include int$keys.h
$include keys.h
$include err.h
* parse.pathname.tokens is declared by keys.h; do not redeclare it.

   twin.list.cap = 200             ;* cap the warning list; the count stays exact

   * CHECK anywhere on the command line means scan and report only, convert
   * nothing.  @sentence is the whole line ("RUN gpl.bp upgrade_nocase ..."),
   * so a token test, not a fixed field position.  The installer runs it
   * without CHECK, so it converts; CHECK is for a dry run by hand.
   check.only = @false
   if index(' ' : upcase(@sentence) : ' ', ' CHECK ', 1) then check.only = @true

   crt sysmsg(10180)               ;* Checking every file for ids that differ only by case...

   scanned = 0                     ;* DH files examined
   converted = 0                   ;* rebuilt to NOCASE
   twin.count = 0                  ;* files holding a fold-pair, NOT converted
   dup.total = 0                   ;* duplicate record ids across all those files
   twin.files = ''                 ;* @fm: one path per file that has a pair
   twin.dups  = ''                 ;* @fm parallel: that file's pairs, @vm joined
   ak.skipped = 0                  ;* AK files left for manual CONFIGURE.FILE
   err.count = 0                   ;* files that could not be read or rebuilt
   sds.count = 0                   ;* files a VOC names under SDSYS: left
   sds.files = ''                  ;* @fm: their real paths (capped like the twin list)
   * Real paths are compared upper cased with one separator: NTFS folds case, and @sdsys carries backslashes where @ds is '/'.
   sds.real = ospath(@sdsys, OS$FULLPATH)
   if sds.real = '' then sds.real = @sdsys
   sds.real = upcase(convert('\', '/', sds.real))
   sds.raw = upcase(convert('\', '/', @sdsys))

   this.is.sdsys = @false
   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'accounts' to acc.f then
      select acc.f to 12
      loop
         readnext reg.name from 12 else exit
         read acc.rec from acc.f, reg.name then
            acc.path = parse.pathname.tokens(acc.rec<ACC$PATH>)
            * SDSYS is detected by its register NAME and skipped entirely: its files are made fresh by the install, after
            * the NOCASE kernel, and its live VOC is the one file this must never rebuild.
            if acc.path # '' and downcase(reg.name) # 'sdsys' then
               crt sysmsg(12035, reg.name)   ;* Account %1
               this.acct = acc.path
               gosub do.account.at
            end
         end
      repeat
      close acc.f
   end else
      crt sysmsg(2200)             ;* Cannot open accounts register
   end

   * ---- report -----------------------------------------------------------
   * File name, then every duplicate record id in it, so an administrator can
   * find and resolve each one.  This is the "report" the pre-check produces;
   * the installer captures it to the log it leaves behind.
   crt
   if twin.count then
      crt sysmsg(10178, twin.count, dup.total)   ;* WARNING: %1 file(s) hold %2 record id(s) that differ only by case. They were NOT converted:
      nfiles = dcount(twin.files, @fm)
      for i = 1 to nfiles
         crt sysmsg(10184, twin.files<i>)        ;* File: %1
         pairs = twin.dups<i>
         np = dcount(pairs, @vm)
         for j = 1 to np
            crt '      ' : pairs<1, j>           ;* "id-one / id-other"
         next j
      next i
      crt sysmsg(10181)            ;* Rename or delete one id of each pair, then convert that file with CONFIGURE.FILE NO.CASE.
   end
   if sds.count then
      crt sysmsg(15993, sds.count)             ;* %1 file(s) named by an account's VOC lie under SDSYS and were left as they are:
      for i = 1 to dcount(sds.files, @fm)
         crt sysmsg(10184, sds.files<i>)       ;* File: %1
      next i
   end
   if check.only then
      crt sysmsg(10182, scanned, twin.count)   ;* Checked %1 file(s); %2 hold a case-only duplicate record id.
   end else
      crt sysmsg(10179, converted, scanned)    ;* Converted %1 of %2 file(s) to case insensitive ids.
   end
   if ak.skipped then crt sysmsg(10186, ak.skipped)  ;* %1 indexed file(s) were left for manual CONFIGURE.FILE; see above.
   if err.count then crt sysmsg(10183, err.count)  ;* %1 file(s) could not be read or rebuilt; see above.
   crt 'COMPLETE'
   stop

* ==========================================================================
* do.account.at - convert every data/dict file named by this.acct's VOC, then
*   the VOC itself.  this.acct is the account path.
do.account.at:
   file.list = ''
   seen.list = ''                  ;* paths already visited in THIS account (see convert.one)
* ON ERROR: a failed open of a DH file's primary subfile gives a NEGATIVE status, and without ON ERROR that is a
*   runtime abort (op_dio1.c exit_op_open), not the ELSE branch.  It is reported (12013) and counted, and the walk
*   goes on.
   openpath this.acct:@ds:'voc' to acct.voc on error
      crt sysmsg(12013, this.acct:@ds:'voc', status())   ;* Cannot open %1 (error %2). It was left as it is.
      err.count += 1
      return
   end else return                 ;* no VOC at this path; nothing to do
* LIST 11, NOT 13.  Select lists run 0 to 12 (HIGH_SELECT, gplsrc/sd.h), and 11-12 only in an $internal program.  The
*   register walk holds 12 for its whole loop, so every inner list here is 11, used one after another.
   select acct.voc to 11
   loop
      readnext vid from 11 else exit
      read vrec from acct.voc, vid then
         if upcase(vrec[1,1]) = 'F' then
            gosub add.file
            if vrec<3> # '' and vrec<3> # vrec<2> then gosub add.file.dict
         end
      end
   repeat
   close acct.voc

   nf = dcount(file.list, @fm)
   for fi = 1 to nf
      one.path = file.list<fi>
      gosub convert.one
   next fi

   * the VOC last, and only when it is not the session's own
   if not(this.is.sdsys) then
      one.path = this.acct:@ds:'voc'
      gosub convert.one
   end
   return

* add.file / add.file.dict - resolve an F-record path (field 2 / field 3) to an
*   absolute pathname and add it to file.list.  A bare name is relative to the
*   account; @SDSYS and the like are expanded by parse.pathname.tokens.
add.file:
   raw = vrec<2>
   gosub resolve.path
   if resolved # '' then file.list<-1> = resolved
   return
add.file.dict:
   raw = vrec<3>
   gosub resolve.path
   if resolved # '' then file.list<-1> = resolved
   return

resolve.path:
   resolved = ''
   if raw = '' then return
   if index(raw, '@', 1) then
      resolved = parse.pathname.tokens(raw)
   end else if raw[1,1] = @ds or index(raw, ':', 1) = 2 then
      resolved = raw               ;* already absolute
   end else
      resolved = this.acct:@ds:raw ;* relative to the account
   end
   return

* ==========================================================================
* convert.one - convert the DH file at one.path to NOCASE, if it needs it and
*   is safe.  Scans for a fold-pair first (phase 1); converts only when clean
*   (phase 2, unless check.only).  Never loses a record: the original is
*   deleted only after a full copy and rename succeed.
convert.one:
* One separator in every path this reports: @sdsys and a register path carry backslashes and @ds is '/'.
   one.path = convert('\', '/', one.path)
* THE PATH IS JUDGED BEFORE THE FILE IS OPENED (Linux Solo's convert.one).  A VOC can name SDSYS's own files ($map,
*   $ipc, voc.dic ...), which the walk has no business opening.  The test is made on the real path; a file under
*   SDSYS is listed once however many records name it, and a path with no %0 beside it (a directory file, or nothing
*   there) is returned silently.
   one.real = ospath(one.path, OS$FULLPATH)
   if one.real = '' then one.real = one.path
   one.real = convert('\', '/', one.real)
   one.key = upcase(one.real)
   if not(ospath(one.real : '/%0', OS$EXISTS)) then return   ;* a directory file, or nothing there: silent
   in.sds = (one.key[1, len(sds.real) + 1] = sds.real : '/') or (one.key[1, len(sds.raw) + 1] = sds.raw : '/')
   if in.sds then
* 21 Nov 26 SD Core Solo - WINDOWS ROW 377, as Linux Solo c2d5f21 (S.296, from SD Core 2ca433b7): a file that is ALREADY case insensitive has
*   nothing left to convert, and SDSYS's own files are, from creation.  Naming them made the closing warning "Some files were not converted"
*   appear on every Keep reinstall (VM sit12).  The flag is read through a READ ONLY open, which changes nothing, so the file is still never
*   converted from here; an open that fails leaves the file counted and named, as before.
      is.nc = 0
      openpath one.path readonly to chk.fu on error
         is.nc = 0
      end then
         is.nc = bitand(fileinfo(chk.fu, FL$FLAGS), FL$FLAGS.NOCASE)
         close chk.fu
      end else
         is.nc = 0
      end
      if is.nc then return
      locate one.real in sds.files<1> setting sds.at else
         sds.count += 1
         if dcount(sds.files, @fm) < twin.list.cap then sds.files<-1> = one.real
      end
      return
   end
* ON ERROR, for the same reason as the VOC open in do.account.at.
   openpath one.path to old.fu on error
      crt sysmsg(12013, one.path, status())   ;* Cannot open %1 (error %2). It was left as it is.
      err.count += 1
      return
   end else return    ;* not a file we can open; skip
   ftype = fileinfo(old.fu, FL$TYPE)
   if ftype # FL$TYPE.DH then
      close old.fu
      return                       ;* directory files fold on NTFS already
   end
* old.path is set here, before phase 1 uses it.
   old.path = fileinfo(old.fu, FL$PATH)
* And the session's own live VOC is never rebuilt, however it was queued.  The test is on the opened file's path,
*   not on how the path was spelled.
   if upcase(old.path) = upcase(fileinfo(@voc, FL$PATH)) then
      close old.fu
      return
   end
* ONE VISIT PER FILE PER ACCOUNT, so "Converted N of M" counts distinct files: an account's own 'voc' is named by
*   its own voc record and is then converted again as the last step.  The key is the opened file's path, separators
*   folded and upper cased, because NTFS folds case.
   chk.path = convert('\', '/', old.path)
   seen.key = upcase(chk.path)
   locate seen.key in seen.list<1> setting seen.at then
      close old.fu
      return
   end
   seen.list<-1> = seen.key
   scanned += 1

   if bitand(fileinfo(old.fu, FL$FLAGS), FL$FLAGS.NOCASE) then
      close old.fu
      return
   end

   * ---- phase 1: scan for EVERY fold-pair, read only ----------------------
   * A temporary case-INSENSITIVE probe file does the duplicate detection with
   * the hash, so this is O(records), not O(records squared): each id is read
   * from the probe (which folds), and a hit whose stored spelling differs is a
   * duplicate.  Every duplicate is recorded, so the report names each one, not
   * only the first.  Record ids only - readnext returns keys, never field
   * data.  The probe holds one record per distinct id and is deleted here.
   dups = ''                       ;* @vm: "spelling-one / spelling-other"
   scan.dir = field(old.path, @ds, 1, dcount(old.path, @ds) - 1)
   scan.path = scan.dir : @ds : '~SDSCAN.' : @userno
   openpath scan.path to scan.fu then
      close scan.fu
      void ospath(scan.path, OS$DELETE)
   end
   create.file scan.path dynamic flags FL$FLAGS.NOCASE on error null
   openpath scan.path to scan.fu else
      crt sysmsg(6170) : ' (' : scan.path : ')'
      crt sysmsg(10184, one.path)   ;* File: %1 - the file that was left, not only the work file that failed
      close old.fu
      err.count += 1
      return
   end
   select old.fu to 11             ;* 11 - see do.account.at
   loop
      readnext sid from 11 else exit
      read prev.spelling from scan.fu, sid then
         if prev.spelling # sid then
            dups<1, -1> = prev.spelling : ' / ' : sid
            dup.total += 1
         end
      end else
         write sid to scan.fu, sid   ;* store the first spelling under its folded key
      end
   repeat
   clearselect 11
   close scan.fu
   void ospath(scan.path, OS$DELETE)

   if dups # '' then
      twin.count += 1
      if dcount(twin.files, @fm) < twin.list.cap then
         twin.files<-1> = one.path
         twin.dups<-1>  = dups
      end
      close old.fu
      return                       ;* leave it exactly as it is
   end

   * A file with alternate key indices is NOT rebuilt here.  Recreating an AK
   * by path needs a BUILD.INDEX that resolves the file in the current
   * account's VOC, which this by-path walk cannot do; a half-built index is
   * worse than none.  So it is reported for the administrator to convert with
   * CONFIGURE.FILE (which rebuilds AKs correctly), and left case sensitive.
   if fileinfo(old.fu, FL$AK) then
      ak.skipped += 1
      crt sysmsg(10185, one.path)   ;* File has indices; convert it with CONFIGURE.FILE NO.CASE: %1
      close old.fu
      return
   end

   if check.only then
      close old.fu
      return
   end

   * ---- phase 2: rebuild NOCASE by path (mirrors CONFIGF convert.file) -----
   * Exclusive access, so nothing else is mid-write.  A file in use is left.
   if not(fileinfo(old.fu, FL$EXCLUSIVE)) then
      crt sysmsg(10168, one.path, fileinfo(old.fu, FL$HOLDERS))
      close old.fu
      err.count += 1
      return
   end

   new.path = field(old.path, @ds, 1, dcount(old.path, @ds) - 1) : @ds : '~SDNOCASE.' : @userno

   openpath new.path to new.fu then
      close new.fu
      if not(ospath(new.path, OS$DELETE)) then
         crt sysmsg(6168) : ' (' : new.path : ')'
         crt sysmsg(10184, one.path)   ;* File: %1 - and the file it left
         close old.fu
         err.count += 1
         return
      end
   end

   create.file new.path dynamic flags FL$FLAGS.NOCASE on error null
   openpath new.path to new.fu else
      crt sysmsg(6170) : ' (' : new.path : ')'
      crt sysmsg(10184, one.path)   ;* File: %1 - and the file it left
      close old.fu
      err.count += 1
      return
   end
   filelock new.fu

   copy.failed = @false
   select old.fu to 11             ;* 11 - see do.account.at
   loop
      readnext cid from 11 else exit
      read crec from old.fu, cid then
         write crec to new.fu, cid
      end else
         copy.failed = @true
         exit
      end
   repeat
   clearselect 11

   if copy.failed then
      close new.fu
      void ospath(new.path, OS$DELETE)
      close old.fu
      crt sysmsg(6171) : ' (' : one.path : ')'
      err.count += 1
      return
   end

   * Carry the trigger across (AK files never reach here - they were reported
   * and skipped above).
   trigger = fileinfo(old.fu, FL$TRIGGER)
   if trigger # '' then
      set.trigger new.fu, trigger, fileinfo(old.fu, FL$TRG.MODES)
   end

   * The counter of WRITE ... CREATING.SEQKEY goes to the new file, as CONFIGURE.FILE's rebuild carries it (it lives
   *   in the file's header; the new file's counter would start again at 1).  FL$SEQ.KEY is the id last given out.
   seq.last = fileinfo(old.fu, FL$SEQ.KEY)
   if seq.last > 0 then void fcontrol.int(new.fu, FCI$SET.SEQ.KEY, seq.last + 1)

   close new.fu
   void ospath('', OS$FLUSH.CACHE)

   close old.fu
   void ospath('', OS$FLUSH.CACHE)

   if not(ospath(old.path, OS$DELETE)) then
      crt sysmsg(6178, status(), os.error()) : ' (' : old.path : ')'
      err.count += 1
      return                       ;* original still in place; new left beside it
   end
   if not(osrename(new.path, old.path)) then
      crt sysmsg(6179, status(), os.error()) : ' (' : new.path : ' -> ' : old.path : ')'
      err.count += 1
      return
   end
   converted += 1
   return

end
